+# Run Java and C++ batch programs with Ray
+
+This guide runs compiled Java and C++ programs from Ray Python tasks and checks their integer statistics against Python. Use this pattern when you have a trusted executable with a small batch interface and don't need Ray's native {doc}`cross-language APIs <../cross-language>`.
+
+<!-- Maintainers: Ray CI doesn't test this end-to-end guide. Full-guide validation is pending. Before submission, run every step on a normal Linux host and replace this comment with the Ray version verified by the human contributor. -->
+
+## Prepare a local directory
+
+Use Linux with Python 3.11 or 3.12, a full Java Development Kit version 17 or later, and a C++17 compiler available as `g++`. Keep Ray authentication enabled and run on a trusted network. Starting a local instance doesn't provide network isolation.
+
+Create an empty directory for the three source files below. Install Ray in a virtual environment:
+
+```bash
+mkdir ray-polyglot-example
+cd ray-polyglot-example
+python -m venv .venv
+. .venv/bin/activate
+python -m pip install 'ray==2.60.0'
+```
+
+## Define the worker protocol
+
+Each process reads one request from standard input and exits after writing one JSON result. The request starts with `RAYPOLY1`, followed by a count and that many integers, separated by whitespace. The workers reject requests larger than 65,536 bytes, batches larger than 4096 integers, and values outside the inclusive range from -1,000,000 to 1,000,000. These bounds keep each batch's sum and sum of squares within a signed 64-bit integer.
+
+Save the following program as `stats_worker.cpp`:
+
+:::{dropdown} C++ worker source
+```cpp
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
+#include <charconv>
+#include <cstdint>
+#include <iostream>
+#include <sstream>
+#include <stdexcept>
+#include <string>
+#include <vector>
+
+static std::int64_t number(const std::string &token) {
+    const auto offset = (!token.empty() && token[0] == '-') ? 1U : 0U;
+    if (token.size() <= offset || (token.size() > offset + 1 && token[offset] == '0')) {
+        throw std::invalid_argument("number");
+    }
+    for (std::size_t i = offset; i < token.size(); ++i) {
+        if (token[i] < '0' || token[i] > '9') throw std::invalid_argument("number");
+    }
+    std::int64_t value = 0;
+    const auto parsed = std::from_chars(token.data(), token.data() + token.size(), value);
+    if (parsed.ec != std::errc{} || parsed.ptr != token.data() + token.size()) {
+        throw std::invalid_argument("number");
+    }
+    return value;
+}
+
+int main() {
+    try {
+        std::string input;
+        char c = 0;
+        while (std::cin.get(c)) {
+            auto byte = static_cast<unsigned char>(c);
+            if (input.size() == 65536 || byte > 127 ||
+                (byte < 32 && c != '\t' && c != '\n' && c != '\r')) {
+                throw std::invalid_argument("request");
+            }
+            input.push_back(c);
+        }
+        std::istringstream stream(input);
+        std::vector<std::string> tokens;
+        std::string token;
+        while (stream >> token) tokens.push_back(token);
+        if (tokens.size() < 2 || tokens[0] != "RAYPOLY1") {
+            throw std::invalid_argument("request");
+        }
+        auto n = number(tokens[1]);
+        if (n < 0 || n > 4096 || tokens.size() != static_cast<std::size_t>(n + 2)) {
+            throw std::invalid_argument("count");
+        }
+        std::int64_t sum = 0;
+        std::int64_t squares = 0;
+        for (std::size_t i = 2; i < tokens.size(); ++i) {
+            auto value = number(tokens[i]);
+            if (value < -1000000 || value > 1000000) throw std::invalid_argument("value");
+            sum += value;
+            squares += value * value;
+        }
+        std::cout << "{\"protocol\":\"RAYPOLY1\",\"language\":\"cpp\",\"count\":" << n
+                  << ",\"sum\":" << sum << ",\"sum_squares\":" << squares << "}\n";
+        return 0;
+    } catch (const std::exception &) {
+        std::cerr << "invalid request\n";
+        return 2;
+    }
+}
+```
+:::
+
+Save the next program as `StatsWorker.java`:
+
+:::{dropdown} Java worker source
+```java
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
+import java.io.IOException;
+import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
+
+/** Independent exact-integer worker. No Ray native bindings or dependencies. */
+public final class StatsWorker {
+    private static long number(String token) {
+        if (!token.matches("-?(0|[1-9][0-9]*)")) {
+            throw new IllegalArgumentException();
+        }
+        return Long.parseLong(token);
+    }
+
+    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
+        try {
+            byte[] bytes = System.in.readNBytes(65537);
+            if (bytes.length > 65536) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
+            for (byte b : bytes) {
+                if (b < 0 || (b < 32 && b != 9 && b != 10 && b != 13)) {
+                    throw new IllegalArgumentException();
+                }
+            }
+            String[] tokens = new String(bytes, StandardCharsets.US_ASCII).trim().split("\\s+");
+            if (tokens.length < 2 || !tokens[0].equals("RAYPOLY1")) {
+                throw new IllegalArgumentException();
+            }
+            long n = number(tokens[1]);
+            if (n < 0 || n > 4096 || tokens.length != n + 2) {
+                throw new IllegalArgumentException();
+            }
+            long sum = 0;
+            long squares = 0;
+            for (int i = 2; i < tokens.length; i++) {
+                long value = number(tokens[i]);
+                if (value < -1000000 || value > 1000000) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
+                sum += value;
+                squares += value * value;
+            }
+            System.out.printf(java.util.Locale.ROOT, "{\"protocol\":\"RAYPOLY1\",\"language\":\"java\","
+                + "\"count\":%d,\"sum\":%d,\"sum_squares\":%d}%n", n, sum, squares);
+        } catch (IllegalArgumentException ex) {
+            System.err.println("invalid request");
+            System.exit(2);
+        }
+    }
+}
+```
+:::
+
+## Define the Python driver
+
+Save the following file as `polyglot_driver.py`. The `batch_stats` function requests one logical CPU and sets `max_retries=0`. Ray schedules the Python task, and that task starts the selected executable. CPU requests are scheduler accounting, not CPU isolation.
+
+The driver sends at most eight pending tasks by default. It waits for results with `ray.wait`, adds their statistics, and compares the totals with an independent Python calculation. Empty input still produces one empty batch per language.
+
+:::{dropdown} Python driver source
+```python
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
+"""Run only the trusted Java and C++ programs included in this guide."""
+
+import argparse
+import json
+import os
+import shutil
+import subprocess
+from pathlib import Path
+
+import ray
+
+MAX_ITEMS = 4096
+MAX_VALUE = 1_000_000
+
+
+def encode(values):
+    if len(values) > MAX_ITEMS or any(
+        type(value) is not int or abs(value) > MAX_VALUE for value in values
+    ):
+        raise ValueError("Expected at most 4096 integers in [-1000000, 1000000]")
+    return ("RAYPOLY1 " + str(len(values)) + " " + " ".join(map(str, values))).encode(
+        "ascii"
+    )
+
+
+def expected(values):
+    encode(values)
+    return {
+        "count": len(values),
+        "sum": sum(values),
+        "sum_squares": sum(value * value for value in values),
+    }
+
+
+def run_worker(language, values, bundle, timeout_s=10):
+    payload = encode(values)
+    bundle = Path(bundle).resolve()
+    if language == "cpp":
+        command = [str(bundle / "native" / "stats_worker")]
+    elif language == "java":
+        java = shutil.which("java")
+        if java is None:
+            raise RuntimeError("Java is missing on this node")
+        command = [java, "-Xmx64m", "-cp", str(bundle / "java"), "StatsWorker"]
+    else:
+        raise ValueError("Expected java or cpp")
+    try:
+        # Keep the child in the Ray worker's process group. These programs
+        # don't spawn descendants. communicate() drains both output pipes.
+        process = subprocess.run(
+            command, input=payload, capture_output=True, timeout=timeout_s, check=False
+        )
+    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
+        # subprocess.run kills and waits for its direct child on timeout.
+        raise RuntimeError("External worker timed out") from None
+    if process.returncode:
+        # Don't include arbitrary child output in a Ray exception.
+        raise RuntimeError(f"External worker exited with status {process.returncode}")
+    if len(process.stdout) > 1024 or process.stderr:
+        raise RuntimeError("Unexpected worker output")
+    try:
+        result = json.loads(process.stdout)
+    except (UnicodeDecodeError, json.JSONDecodeError):
+        raise RuntimeError("Invalid worker JSON") from None
+    reference = expected(values)
+    if (
+        not isinstance(result, dict)
+        or set(result) != {"protocol", "language", *reference}
+        or result["protocol"] != "RAYPOLY1"
+        or result["language"] != language
+        or any(type(result[key]) is not int for key in reference)
+        or any(result[key] != value for key, value in reference.items())
+    ):
+        raise RuntimeError("Worker result doesn't match the Python baseline")
+    return reference
+
+
+@ray.remote(num_cpus=1, max_retries=0)
+def batch_stats(language, values):
+    stats = run_worker(language, values, Path.cwd())
+    return {
+        "language": language,
+        "stats": stats,
+        "worker_pid": os.getpid(),
+        "node_id": ray.get_runtime_context().get_node_id(),
+    }
+
+
+def compare(values, batch_size=256, window=8):
+    if type(batch_size) is not int or not 1 <= batch_size <= MAX_ITEMS:
+        raise ValueError("Invalid batch size")
+    if type(window) is not int or not 1 <= window <= 128:
+        raise ValueError("Invalid pending-task limit")
+    # Validate all inputs before submitting any tasks.
+    for start in range(0, max(1, len(values)), batch_size):
+        encode(values[start : start + batch_size])
+    totals = {
+        language: {"count": 0, "sum": 0, "sum_squares": 0}
+        for language in ("java", "cpp")
+    }
+    baseline = {
+        "count": len(values),
+        "sum": sum(values),
+        "sum_squares": sum(value * value for value in values),
+    }
+    pending = []
+    worker_pids = set()
+    node_ids = set()
+    completed = 0
+
+    def collect():
+        nonlocal completed
+        ready, rest = ray.wait(pending, num_returns=1, timeout=60)
+        if not ready:
+            raise TimeoutError("No Ray task completed within 60 seconds")
+        pending[:] = rest
+        result = ray.get(ready[0])
+        completed += 1
+        worker_pids.add(result["worker_pid"])
+        node_ids.add(result["node_id"])
+        for key, value in result["stats"].items():
+            totals[result["language"]][key] += value
+
+    try:
+        for start in range(0, max(1, len(values)), batch_size):
+            batch = values[start : start + batch_size]
+            for language in totals:
+                pending.append(batch_stats.remote(language, batch))
+                if len(pending) >= window:
+                    collect()
+        while pending:
+            collect()
+    except BaseException:
+        for ref in pending:
+            ray.cancel(ref, force=False)
+        raise
+    if any(total != baseline for total in totals.values()):
+        raise RuntimeError("Aggregated results don't match")
+    return {
+        "verified": True,
+        "totals": totals,
+        "task_count": completed,
+        "worker_pids": sorted(worker_pids),
+        "node_ids": sorted(node_ids),
+    }
+
+
+def main():
+    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
+    parser.add_argument("--bundle", type=Path, required=True)
+    parser.add_argument("--direct", action="store_true")
+    args = parser.parse_args()
+    values = list(range(-2048, 2048))
+    if args.direct:
+        print(
+            json.dumps(
+                {
+                    lang: run_worker(lang, values, args.bundle)
+                    for lang in ("java", "cpp")
+                }
+            )
+        )
+        return
+    # Start a new local instance rather than connecting to an existing cluster.
+    ray.init(
+        address="local",
+        num_cpus=2,
+        include_dashboard=False,
+        runtime_env={"working_dir": str(args.bundle.resolve())},
+    )
+    try:
+        print(json.dumps(compare(values), indent=2))
+    finally:
+        ray.shutdown()
+
+
+if __name__ == "__main__":
+    main()
+```
+:::
+
+## Compile and stage the artifacts
+
+Run these commands in the directory containing the three source files. They create a fresh bundle, compile the Java and C++ programs, and copy only the driver module. The fresh directory avoids shipping unrelated source files, credentials, or previous build outputs.
+
+```bash
+set -eu
+bundle="$(mktemp -d "$PWD/polyglot-bundle.XXXXXX")"
+mkdir "$bundle/native" "$bundle/java"
+g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -pedantic \
+    stats_worker.cpp -o "$bundle/native/stats_worker"
+javac --release 17 -Xlint:all -Werror -d "$bundle/java" StatsWorker.java
+cp polyglot_driver.py "$bundle/"
+printf '%s\n' "$bundle"
+```
+
+Keep the `bundle` variable in the same shell for the next commands. Ray sends this directory to its workers through `runtime_env.working_dir`. The Java runtime and the C++ executable's dynamic libraries must already exist on every node. Compiling a native executable on one machine doesn't establish compatibility with another operating system, architecture, or C library.
+
+## Check the programs without Ray
+
+Run both executables directly through the driver first:
+
+```bash
+python polyglot_driver.py --bundle "$bundle" --direct
+```
+
+Both programs return these statistics for the integers from -2048 through 2047:
+
+```json
+{"count": 4096, "sum": -2048, "sum_squares": 5726623744}
+```
+
+This check isolates compiler, Java runtime, native-library, and protocol errors before you start Ray.
+
+## Run the Ray tasks
+
+Run the driver without `--direct`:
+
+```bash
+python polyglot_driver.py --bundle "$bundle"
+```
+
+The driver starts a new local Ray instance with two CPU scheduling slots. It divides 4096 integers into 16 batches and submits each batch to both languages, for 32 tasks. A successful run returns `verified: true`, matching totals, and the Python worker process IDs and node IDs. Those process IDs identify the Ray workers, not the Java or C++ child processes. Different worker processes on one node don't establish multi-machine execution or a performance improvement.
+
+The script shuts down its local Ray instance in a `finally` block. To remove the generated artifacts afterward, delete only the temporary directory printed by the compile step.
+
+## Understand the operating limits
+
+The example starts a JVM for every Java batch. Startup cost can dominate small workloads. The `-Xmx64m` setting limits the Java heap, not total resident memory. Measure your own workload before choosing batch sizes or adding persistent workers.
+
+:::{caution}
+Run only the two trusted programs included in this guide. The driver buffers process output in memory and checks its size after the child exits. That check isn't a hard memory limit for a hostile or modified executable. The adapter isn't a security sandbox, and child processes inherit the Ray worker's environment.
+:::
+
+`subprocess.run` kills and reaps its direct child after a subprocess timeout. These programs don't spawn descendants. The driver doesn't create a new process session, so it doesn't intentionally detach the children from Ray's worker process group. See {doc}`Lifetimes of user-spawned processes <../user-spawn-processes>` for cleanup behavior when a Ray worker exits. This guide doesn't validate worker-crash cleanup or provide an orphan-free guarantee.
+
+If a task fails, the driver requests cooperative cancellation of the remaining tasks. Cancellation isn't immediate termination of native code. The per-process timeout and the wait for a completed task serve different purposes, and neither is a hard overall job deadline.
+
+Automatic task retries are disabled for this example. This isn't an exactly-once execution guarantee. Define idempotency and recovery behavior before adapting the pattern to programs with external side effects.
+
+The example uses a local instance. Multi-node deployment, native Ray language APIs, worker-crash behavior, and throughput remain separate validation tasks. For dependency packaging, see {doc}`Handling dependencies <../handling-dependencies>`.
